      <Value><![CDATA[# Two Paths to Content

When designing the Blog Post document type, I faced a choice:

- **Markdown Editor**: Plain text with formatting syntax
- **Rich Text Editor (Tiptap)**: WYSIWYG interface

Both are valid. Both ship with Umbraco. Here's why I chose Markdown.

## The Case for Rich Text

**Pros:**
- Visual editing
- No syntax to learn
- Insert images/links with UI
- Familiar to non-technical users

**Cons:**
- HTML output can be messy
- Harder to version control
- Easy to introduce inconsistent styling
- Editor might add unexpected markup

## The Case for Markdown

**Pros:**
- Clean, predictable output
- Human-readable source
- Version control friendly
- Enforces consistent structure
- Future-proof (it's just text)

**Cons:**
- Requires learning syntax
- No visual preview (unless editor supports it)
- Can't easily embed complex HTML

## Why I Chose Markdown

Three reasons:

### 1. Predictability

Markdown compiles to clean, semantic HTML:

```markdown
# Heading
Paragraph with **bold** and *italic*.
```

Becomes:

```html
<h1>Heading</h1>
<p>Paragraph with <strong>bold</strong> and <em>italic</em>.</p>
```

No surprise `<div>`s or inline styles. Just clean markup.

### 2. Portability

Markdown files can move between systems:
- Static site generators (Hugo, Jekyll)
- Documentation platforms (Read the Docs)
- Note-taking apps (Obsidian, Notion)
- Git repositories (GitHub, GitLab)

It's a universal format. Rich text HTML is tied to the specific editor that created it.

### 3. Version Control

This is a git-tracked demo. Markdown content diffs clearly:

```diff
- This is the old sentence.
+ This is the new sentence.
```

Rich text diffs are noisy:

```diff
- <p><span style="font-size: 14px">This is the old sentence.</span></p>
+ <p><span style="font-size: 14px">This is the new sentence.</span></p>
```

Same change, worse signal-to-noise ratio.

## The Trade-Off

I'm trading **convenience** for **control**.

Rich text is easier for non-technical editors. Markdown is easier for developers and version control.

Since this is a technical demo at a developer conference, Markdown aligns with the audience.

## Umbraco's Flexibility

The beautiful thing? Umbraco doesn't force the choice.

I could:
- Use Markdown for blog posts
- Use Rich Text for landing pages
- Use both on the same document type in different fields

Data types are modular. Mix and match as needed.

## What This Reveals

Editor choices reflect **trust models**:

- **Rich Text**: "Trust the editor to make good choices"
- **Markdown**: "Trust the syntax to enforce structure"

Neither is wrong. They optimize for different scenarios.

## For Content Editors

If you're a content editor reading this and thinking "I hate Markdown":

That's valid! Many excellent sites use rich text editors.

But consider learning Markdown. Once you internalize the syntax (takes about an hour), it's incredibly fast:

- `**bold**` vs clicking a button
- `[link](url)` vs opening a dialog
- `## heading` vs dropdown selection

It's keyboard-driven. No mouse required. For high-volume writing, that's a productivity win.

## The Bottom Line

Markdown won for this site because:
1. Clean output
2. Git-friendly
3. Developer audience

Your site might choose differently. And that's the power of Umbraco—the choice is yours.]]></Value>
    </content>
    <excerpt>
      <Value><![CDATA[The choice between Markdown and rich text editors isn't just technical—it's philosophical. Here's why I picked Markdown.]]></Value>
